# How to work

- Judge only from the cluster material in the user message: its frequent terms and the titles and abstracts of its most central records.
- Ground every claim in quotes: copy, verbatim, the words of the abstracts that show what these studies examine and how they differ from the target. A quote is a copy of words from a record, never a sentence of your own. Give the record id with each quote.
- If the records do not form one literature, or if they look like genuine candidates for the review rather than a neighbouring field, set `is_adjacent` to false and explain in `differs_from_construct`.

# Output

One JSON object with:
- `is_adjacent`: true or false
- `already_listed`: true if the cluster is one of the literatures already listed
- `key`: lower-case identifier with underscores
- `name`: the name researchers in that tradition would use for their own field
- `description`: two or three sentences saying what these studies examine, compare and measure, in the register of their own abstracts
- `differs_from_construct`: one sentence naming what the target studies require that this literature lacks, or what it adds that the target excludes
- `discriminating_cue`: the words or framing in a title or abstract that tell a record of this literature apart from an include
- `example_phrases`: three to five short phrases typical of these abstracts
- `quotes`: two to four objects `{record_id, quote}`, each quote copied verbatim from that record's title or abstract
